{"task_id": "Mbpp/84", "solution": "def sequence(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the nth number in the Newman-Conway sequence using memoization.\n    Raises ValueError if n is less than 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 1:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer (n >= 1).\")\n\n    # Use a dictionary for memoization to store previously computed values.\n    # The cache is defined inside the function's scope but persists across calls\n    # using a mutable default argument or by making it a global/nonlocal variable.\n    # For a simple function, an outer scope dictionary or a static attribute on a class\n    # would work. Here, we'll simulate the class design's caching within the function's scope.\n    if not hasattr(sequence, \"_cache\"):\n        sequence._cache = {} # Initialize cache on first call\n\n    _cache = sequence._cache\n\n    if n in _cache:\n        return _cache[n]\n\n    if n == 1 or n == 2:\n        _cache[n] = 1\n        return 1\n\n    # Recursive definition: P(n) = P(P(n-1)) + P(n-P(n-1))\n    val_n_minus_1 = sequence(n - 1)\n    \n    # P(P(n-1))\n    p_of_val_n_minus_1 = sequence(val_n_minus_1)\n    \n    # P(n - P(n-1))\n    val_n_minus_p_of_val_n_minus_1 = sequence(n - val_n_minus_1)\n\n    result = p_of_val_n_minus_1 + val_n_minus_p_of_val_n_minus_1\n    _cache[n] = result\n    return result"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/90", "solution": "def len_log(words: list[str]) -> int:\n    # Calculates the length of the longest word in a list of words.\n    # Returns 0 if the list is empty.\n    if not words:\n        return 0\n\n    max_length = 0\n    for word in words:\n        current_word_length = len(word)\n        if current_word_length > max_length:\n            max_length = current_word_length\n            \n    return max_length"}
