{"task_id": "Mbpp/119", "solution": "def search(array: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the single element that appears only once in a sorted array,\n    where all other elements appear exactly twice.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(array)\n    low = 0\n    high = n - 1\n\n    while low < high:\n        mid = low + (high - low) // 2\n\n        # Ensure mid points to an even index.\n        # If mid is odd, decrement it to point to the start of a potential pair.\n        if mid % 2 == 1:\n            mid -= 1\n\n        # Check if the pair (array[mid], array[mid+1]) is intact.\n        if array[mid] == array[mid+1]:\n            # The pair is intact, so the unique element must be to the right.\n            # Move low past this pair.\n            low = mid + 2\n        else:\n            # The pair is broken, meaning array[mid] is the unique element\n            # or the unique element is to its left.\n            # Reduce the search space to [low, mid].\n            high = mid\n            \n    # When the loop terminates, low (and high) points to the index of the unique element.\n    return array[low]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/120", "solution": "def max_product_tuple(list_of_tuples):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum absolute product between numbers in pairs of tuples within a given list.\n    Returns 0 if the list is empty or contains no valid tuples.\n    \"\"\"\n    max_absolute_product = 0.0\n\n    for item in list_of_tuples:\n        # Assuming item is always a tuple of length 2 as per the problem description context and example.\n        # Design context explicitly mentions validation, but problem requirements ask to skip isinstance() checks and exception raising.\n        # Following the strict output requirements, validation logic for isinstance and len is omitted, assuming well-formed input items for this specific implementation based on the prompt's constraints.\n        num1, num2 = item\n        current_product = num1 * num2\n        current_absolute_product = abs(current_product)\n\n        if current_absolute_product > max_absolute_product:\n            max_absolute_product = current_absolute_product\n\n    return max_absolute_product"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/126", "solution": "def sum_common_divisors(num1: int, num2: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the sum of common divisors for two positive integers.\"\"\"\n\n    def _get_divisors(n: int) -> set[int]:\n        \"\"\"Calculates all positive divisors for a given positive integer n.\"\"\"\n        divs = set()\n        # Iterate from 1 up to the square root of n to find divisors efficiently.\n        for i in range(1, int(n**0.5) + 1):\n            if n % i == 0:\n                divs.add(i)\n                divs.add(n // i)\n        return divs\n\n    # Get divisors for both numbers.\n    divisors1 = _get_divisors(num1)\n    divisors2 = _get_divisors(num2)\n\n    # Find the common divisors by taking the intersection of the two sets.\n    common_divisors = divisors1.intersection(divisors2)\n\n    # Calculate and return the sum of the common divisors.\n    return sum(common_divisors)"}
