{"task_id": "Mbpp/244", "solution": "def next_Perfect_Square(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Finds the next perfect square strictly greater than the given number. Returns 0 for negative inputs.\"\"\"\n    import math\n\n    if number < 0:\n        return 0\n\n    sqrt_val = math.isqrt(number)\n    next_root = sqrt_val + 1\n    return next_root * next_root"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "def max_sum(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence for the given array.\n    A bitonic subsequence is first strictly increasing and then strictly decreasing.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return arr[0]\n\n    # Calculate LIS_sum[i]: max sum of an increasing subsequence ending at arr[i]\n    lis_sum = list(arr)\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                lis_sum[i] = max(lis_sum[i], lis_sum[j] + arr[i])\n\n    # Calculate LDS_sum[i]: max sum of a decreasing subsequence starting at arr[i]\n    # We can do this by reversing the array conceptually and finding LIS,\n    # or by iterating backwards\n    lds_sum = list(arr)\n    for i in range(n - 2, -1, -1):\n        for j in range(n - 1, i, -1):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j]:\n                lds_sum[i] = max(lds_sum[i], lds_sum[j] + arr[i])\n\n    # Combine LIS_sum and LDS_sum to find the maximum bitonic sum\n    # A bitonic subsequence needs a peak. If LIS_sum[i] + LDS_sum[i] is considered,\n    # arr[i] is counted twice, so subtract arr[i].\n    max_bitonic_sum = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        # A bitonic subsequence must have at least one element.\n        # It must also be strictly increasing then strictly decreasing.\n        # This means an element can be a peak (counted in both LIS and LDS).\n        # To avoid double counting the peak element, subtract arr[i].\n        # However, a sequence can be purely increasing or purely decreasing (degenerate bitonic).\n        # We need to ensure that lis_sum[i] and lds_sum[i] are not just arr[i] itself,\n        # indicating a non-increasing/non-decreasing part.\n        # The condition (lis_sum[i] > arr[i] or lds_sum[i] > arr[i]) ensures there's\n        # at least a length of 2 for one side, but it's simpler to just ensure n > 1.\n        # For a single element, lis_sum[i] = arr[i] and lds_sum[i] = arr[i].\n        # Sum is arr[i], and (arr[i] + arr[i] - arr[i]) = arr[i].\n        # This handles the degenerate cases.\n        max_bitonic_sum = max(max_bitonic_sum, lis_sum[i] + lds_sum[i] - arr[i])\n\n    return max_bitonic_sum"}
