{"task_id": "Mbpp/259", "solution": "def maximize_elements(\n    tuple1: tuple[tuple[int, int], ...],\n    tuple2: tuple[tuple[int, int], ...]\n) -> tuple[tuple[int, int], ...]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Maximizes elements of two input tuple collections element-wise.\n    It returns a new tuple of tuples with the maximum of corresponding elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    result_inner_tuples = []\n    for inner_tuple1, inner_tuple2 in zip(tuple1, tuple2):\n        max_element0 = max(inner_tuple1[0], inner_tuple2[0])\n        max_element1 = max(inner_tuple1[1], inner_tuple2[1])\n        result_inner_tuples.append((max_element0, max_element1))\n    return tuple(result_inner_tuples)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/256", "solution": "def count_Primes_nums(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts the number of prime numbers strictly less than the given non-negative number.\n    Uses a helper function to check primality for each number.\n    \"\"\"\n    def _is_prime(num: int) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"\n        Helper function to determine if a given number is prime.\n        \"\"\"\n        if num < 2:\n            return False\n        if num == 2:\n            return True\n        if num % 2 == 0:\n            return False\n        i = 3\n        while i * i <= num:\n            if num % i == 0:\n                return False\n            i += 2\n        return True\n\n    count = 0\n    if number <= 2:\n        return 0\n\n    for i in range(2, number):\n        if _is_prime(i):\n            count += 1\n    return count"}
