{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(number: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Sets the left-most (most significant) unset bit in the given integer.\"\"\"\n    if number == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # Get the number of bits required to represent the absolute value of the number.\n    # For example, 10 (binary 1010) has bit_length() of 4.\n    # We will search for unset bits from index (num_bits - 1) down to 0.\n    num_bits = number.bit_length()\n\n    # Iterate from the most significant bit position down to the least significant bit.\n    for i in range(num_bits - 1, -1, -1):\n        # Check if the i-th bit is unset (0).\n        if not (number & (1 << i)):\n            # If an unset bit is found, set it and return the new number.\n            return number | (1 << i)\n\n    # If the loop completes, all bits from 0 to num_bits-1 are set.\n    # In this case, the left-most unset bit is at the next position (num_bits).\n    # For example, if number is 7 (111), num_bits is 3. The new bit to set is at position 3.\n    return number | (1 << num_bits)"}
