{"task_id": "Mbpp/473", "solution": "class Tuple:\n    \"\"\"\n    Represents a tuple for order-agnostic comparison.\n    It stores the original tuple and provides a canonical form (frozenset of elements)\n    to enable comparison and hashing irrespective of element order.\n    \"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, data: tuple):\n        self._original_tuple = data\n        self._canonical_form = frozenset(data)\n\n    def get_original_tuple(self) -> tuple:\n        \"\"\"Returns the original tuple.\"\"\"\n        return self._original_tuple\n\n    def __hash__(self) -> int:\n        \"\"\"Enables hashing based on the canonical form.\"\"\"\n        return hash(self._canonical_form)\n\n    def __eq__(self, other: object) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Defines equality based on the canonical form for order-agnostic comparison.\"\"\"\n        if not isinstance(other, Tuple):\n            return NotImplemented\n        return self._canonical_form == other._canonical_form\n\n    def __repr__(self) -> str:\n        \"\"\"Provides a string representation for debugging.\"\"\"\n        return f\"Tuple({self._original_tuple!r})\"\n\ndef tuple_intersection(list_of_tuples_1: list[tuple], list_of_tuples_2: list[tuple]) -> set[tuple]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the intersection of elements in two given tuple lists, irrespective of their order.\n    The result is a set of tuples, where each tuple is from an input list that matched.\n    \"\"\"\n    set_of_tuple_objects_1 = {Tuple(t) for t in list_of_tuples_1}\n    set_of_tuple_objects_2 = {Tuple(t) for t in list_of_tuples_2}\n\n    intersection_of_tuple_objects = set_of_tuple_objects_1.intersection(set_of_tuple_objects_2)\n\n    result_set_of_original_tuples = {t_obj.get_original_tuple() for t_obj in intersection_of_tuple_objects}\n\n    return result_set_of_original_tuples"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/476", "solution": "def big_sum(array: list[float | int]) -> float | int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of the largest and smallest values in the given array.\n    Raises ValueError if the input array is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not array:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input array cannot be empty\")\n    \n    # Python's built-in min() and max() functions are efficient for this task.\n    # For a single-element array, min() and max() will return that element,\n    # and their sum will correctly be twice the element's value as per BR3.\n    return min(array) + max(array)"}
